# AUTO-GENERATED by AICaC — do not edit. Source: .ai/decisions.yaml
version: "1.0"
decisions[12]:
  - id: ADR-001
    title: "Dual implementation: workflows + composite actions"
    date: 2024-11-15
    status: accepted
    context: "GitHub Enterprise Server doesn't support cross-repository workflow_call\nfor reusable workflows. Users on GHES cannot use v2.x workflows.\n"
    decision: Maintain both reusable workflows (v2.x) and composite actions (v3.0)
    alternatives_considered[3]{name,rejected_because}:
      Reusable workflows only,Excludes GHES users without github.com access
      Composite actions only,Worse UX for github.com users (more boilerplate)
      "External CI tool (Jenkins, Dagger)","Adds external dependency, less GitHub-native"
    consequences:
      positive[3]: Supports both github.com and isolated GHES,Users can choose implementation that fits their environment,No disruption during migration period
      negative[3]: Higher maintenance burden (two codepaths),Must test both implementations,Documentation complexity
    implementation: "v2.x: .github/workflows/\nv3.0: .github/actions/\n"
  - id: ADR-002
    title: SARIF as universal output format
    date: 2024-11-20
    status: accepted
    context: "Each security scanner has its own output format (JSON, text, XML).\nGitHub Security tab requires SARIF format for integration.\n"
    decision: Convert all scanner outputs to SARIF format
    alternatives_considered[2]{name,rejected_because}:
      Keep native formats,"No unified severity thresholds, no GitHub Security integration"
      Custom unified format,"Not GitHub-native, requires custom tooling"
    consequences:
      positive[4]: Unified severity thresholds across scanners,GitHub Security tab integration,PR annotations for findings,"Industry standard, tooling ecosystem exists"
      negative[2]: Some scanner-specific nuances lost in translation,Requires parse scripts for each scanner
    implementation: Each scanner has scripts/parse.sh that outputs SARIF-compatible JSON
  - id: ADR-003
    title: Bash scripts for parsing over Node.js/Python
    date: 2024-12-01
    status: accepted
    context: "Parse scripts run inside GitHub Actions. Need to convert scanner\noutput to structured JSON quickly and reliably.\n"
    decision: Use bash + jq/yq for parsing scripts
    alternatives_considered[3]{name,rejected_because}:
      Node.js scripts,"Requires npm install step, slower action startup"
      Python scripts,"Requires Python setup, heavier runtime"
      Go binaries,"Compilation step, harder to debug"
    consequences:
      positive[4]: No runtime dependencies to install,Fast action execution,jq/yq commonly available in GitHub runners,Easy to debug (view script inline)
      negative[3]: Less maintainable than typed languages,Limited testing frameworks for bash,Complex string manipulation harder
    implementation: .github/actions/*/scripts/parse.sh
  - id: ADR-004
    title: Co-located tests with actions
    date: 2024-12-05
    status: accepted
    context: "Each composite action needs tests. Question: where should tests live?\n"
    decision: Tests live in .github/actions/*/tests/ alongside the action
    alternatives_considered[2]{name,rejected_because}:
      Centralized tests/ directory,"Tests divorced from code they test, harder to maintain"
      Separate test repository,"Even more divorced, harder to keep in sync"
    consequences:
      positive[4]: Tests next to code they test,Easy to see test coverage at a glance,Natural to update tests when changing action,Self-contained actions (action + tests together)
      negative[2]: Larger action directories,Must traverse multiple directories to run all tests
    implementation: .github/actions/scanner-*/tests/
  - id: ADR-005
    title: Single version.yaml as source of truth
    date: 2024-12-10
    status: accepted
    context: "Version number appears in 20+ files: README, examples, action references,\nCONTRIBUTING docs, etc. Keeping them in sync is error-prone.\n"
    decision: "Use version.yaml as single source, release-it updates all references"
    alternatives_considered[2]{name,rejected_because}:
      Manual version updates,"Error-prone, easy to miss files"
      Git tags only,Docs still need hardcoded versions for examples
    consequences:
      positive[3]: Single place to check/update version,Automated consistency across all files,release-it handles version bumping
      negative[2]: Requires release-it configuration maintenance,Must remember to use release-it (not manual git tag)
    implementation: "version.yaml - source of truth\n.release-it.json - configuration for version replacement patterns\n"
  - id: ADR-006
    title: Matrix strategy for parallel scanner execution
    date: 2024-11-25
    status: accepted
    context: "Running 10+ scanners sequentially is slow. GitHub Actions supports\nmatrix strategy for parallel job execution.\n"
    decision: Use matrix strategy to run scanners in parallel
    alternatives_considered[2]{name,rejected_because}:
      Sequential execution,Too slow (30+ minutes vs 5-10 minutes)
      Single job with all scanners,"No parallelism, harder to isolate failures"
    consequences:
      positive[3]: Much faster execution (parallel),Each scanner isolated (failures don't cascade),Easy to add/remove scanners from matrix
      negative[2]: More complex workflow syntax,Matrix job limit (256) could be hit with many containers
    implementation: "strategy:\n  matrix:\n    scanner: [codeql, bandit, gitleaks, ...]\n  fail-fast: false\n"
  - id: ADR-007
    title: Severity-based failure thresholds
    date: 2024-12-15
    status: accepted
    context: "Different branches need different failure criteria. Main branch should\nfail on critical findings, dev branches might allow medium severity.\n"
    decision: "Implement fail_on_severity input with levels: critical/high/medium/low/none"
    alternatives_considered[3]{name,rejected_because}:
      Always fail on any finding,Too strict for development branches
      Never fail (report only),Defeats purpose of security scanning
      Branch-specific defaults,"User should control, not us"
    consequences:
      positive[3]: User controls failure behavior,Supports different policies for different branches,"Can run report-only scans (fail_on_severity: none)"
      negative[1]: Users might set too lenient thresholds
    implementation: "inputs:\n  fail_on_severity:\n    description: 'Minimum severity to fail workflow'\n    default: 'high'\n    type: choice\n    options: [critical, high, medium, low, none]\n"
  - id: ADR-008
    title: "Flexible PR commenting: standalone vs aggregated"
    date: 2026-02-05
    status: accepted
    context: "Scanner actions can be used individually or via reusable workflows.\nIndividual usage benefits from scanner-specific PR comments.\nMultiple scanners benefit from single aggregated PR comment.\nNeed to support both patterns without PR comment spam.\n"
    decision: "All scanner composite actions include comment-pr step with post_pr_comment\ninput defaulting to false. Reusable workflows set post_pr_comment: false\nfor all scanners, only security-summary job posts comprehensive comment.\n"
    alternatives_considered[3]{name,rejected_because}:
      Always post individual comments,Spams PRs with 10+ comments when using reusable workflow
      Never post individual comments,Poor UX when using scanners individually
      Remove comment logic from actions entirely,Forces users to implement their own PR commenting
    consequences:
      positive[4]: Standalone scanners can post focused PR comments,Reusable workflows produce single comprehensive comment,Users control behavior via post_pr_comment input,No PR comment spam from multiple scanners
      negative[2]: Requires post_pr_comment input on all scanner actions,Must document when to use true vs false
    implementation: "Scanner actions (.github/actions/scanner-*/action.yml):\n  inputs:\n    post_pr_comment:\n      default: 'false'\n  steps:\n    - name: Comment PR\n      if: github.event_name == 'pull_request' && inputs.post_pr_comment == 'true'\n      uses: ./.github/actions/comment-pr\n\nReusable workflow (.github/workflows/reusable-security-hardening.yml):\n  scanner-codeql:\n    with:\n      post_pr_comment: false  # All scanners set to false\n  scanner-opengrep:\n    with:\n      post_pr_comment: false\n  # ... all other scanners with post_pr_comment: false\n\n  security-summary:\n    steps:\n      - name: Comment PR with security summary\n        uses: actions/github-script@v8  # Only job that posts PR comment\n"
    usage_examples:
      standalone: "# Individual scanner posts its own PR comment\n- uses: huntridge-labs/argus/.github/actions/scanner-bandit@0.5.0\n  with:\n    post_pr_comment: true\n"
      reusable: "# Only security-summary posts comprehensive comment\nuses: huntridge-labs/argus/.github/workflows/reusable-security-hardening.yml@0.5.0\nwith:\n  scanners: all\n  post_pr_comment: true  # Controls security-summary, not individual scanners\n"
  - id: ADR-009
    title: Consolidate scripts and tests to Python
    date: 2026-02-08
    status: accepted
    context: "Project maintained three languages for action scripts (Bash, JavaScript, Python)\nplus Ruby for Bash coverage via bashcov/SimpleCov. This fragmentation created:\n- Three test frameworks with different patterns\n- Three coverage tools with incompatible reporting\n- Higher contributor barrier (must learn multiple languages)\n- Harder to maintain and refactor code\n- CI complexity (multiple setup/teardown steps)\n"
    decision: Migrate all action scripts and tests to Python with pytest
    alternatives_considered[3]{name,rejected_because}:
      Keep multi-language approach,Undermines trust in coverage metrics and raises contributor barrier
      Migrate only to Bash,"Bash lacks type safety, harder to test complex logic"
      Migrate only to JavaScript,Adds Node.js dependency; Python already used for some actions
    consequences:
      positive[7]: Single test framework (pytest) with consistent patterns,One coverage tool (pytest-cov) producing unified metrics,"Lower contributor barrier (Python widely known, standard on CI runners)",Better readability (200-line Bash becomes ~80 lines Python),"Simplified CI/CD (no Ruby, no Node.js for tests)","Eliminated duplicate script pairs (container/container-summary, zap/zap-summary)","Shared utility modules (sarif.py, summary.py, severity.py)"
      negative[2]: Initial migration effort across 18 scripts + 15 test files,Python 3.11+ runtime requirement (but pre-installed on ubuntu-latest)
    implementation: "Phase 0: Foundation (.github/actions/_shared/ utilities + pytest.ini)\nPhase 1: Migrate action scripts (.sh → .py) and tests\nPhase 2: Migrate config parsers (.js → .py)\nPhase 3: Clean up (remove old Bash/JS files and coverage tools)\nPhase 4: Harden testing (edge cases, integration tests)\nPhase 5: Update documentation\n"
    supersedes: ADR-003 (Bash for scripts with no runtime dependencies)
    note: "Python is now the lingua franca for action scripts and tests.\nNode.js remains for release tooling (release-it, commitlint, husky) which is\nappropriate as those are developer workflow tools, not pipeline logic.\n"
  - id: ADR-010
    title: Hybrid rule-based + AI classification for FedRAMP SCN detection
    date: 2026-02-21
    status: accepted
    context: "FedRAMP 20X requires classifying IaC changes into SCN categories (Routine,\nAdaptive, Transformative, Impact). Some changes map cleanly to rules (e.g.,\ntag changes are always Routine), but many are ambiguous and require judgment.\nThe project also has an air-gap constraint — some environments cannot make\nexternal API calls.\n"
    decision: "Use a hybrid classification approach:\n1. Rule-based pattern matching first (deterministic, fast, air-gap safe)\n2. AI fallback via Claude Haiku for ambiguous cases (optional, requires API key)\n3. MANUAL_REVIEW category when neither method can classify with confidence\n"
    alternatives_considered[3]{name,rejected_because}:
      Rules only,Cannot handle novel resource types or complex attribute interactions
      AI only,"Violates air-gap constraint, adds latency and cost to every classification"
      ML model trained on FedRAMP data,"Insufficient training data, high maintenance burden, still needs API"
    consequences:
      positive[5]: Works fully offline with rule-based classification,AI improves accuracy for ambiguous cases when available,Graceful degradation — MANUAL_REVIEW instead of wrong classification,Configurable confidence threshold prevents low-confidence AI results,Audit trail records which method was used per classification
      negative[3]: AI fallback requires Anthropic API key and network access,Rule maintenance burden grows with supported IaC providers,Two code paths to test and maintain
    implementation: ".github/actions/scn-detector/scripts/classify_changes.py:\n  - ChangeClassifier.classify_with_rules() — pattern matching\n  - ChangeClassifier.classify_with_ai() — multi-provider AI fallback\n  - classify_change() orchestrates: rules first, AI if unmatched\nProvider abstraction in ai_providers.py (see ADR-011)\nEach provider SDK is optional (try/except ImportError)\nrequests library used as HTTP fallback when SDK unavailable\n"
  - id: ADR-011
    title: Multi-provider AI abstraction for SCN classifier
    date: 2026-02-21
    status: accepted
    context: "ADR-010 established hybrid rule-based + AI classification but hardcoded\nthe AI provider to Anthropic Claude Haiku. Users need to:\n1. Choose their preferred AI model (e.g., claude-3-5-sonnet, gpt-4o-mini)\n2. Choose their preferred provider (Anthropic or OpenAI)\n3. Use OpenAI-compatible APIs (Azure OpenAI, Ollama, vLLM) via custom base URLs\nThe classifier was tightly coupled to Anthropic's API format, SDK, and env vars.\n"
    decision: "Add a lightweight provider abstraction layer in ai_providers.py with:\n1. Registry pattern (PROVIDERS dict) mapping provider names to classes\n2. Factory function (create_provider) for instantiation\n3. Each provider implements call(prompt) → str with SDK-first + HTTP fallback\n4. Provider-aware API key resolution (resolve_api_key)\n5. Config-driven via ai_fallback.provider field in scn-config.yml\n"
    alternatives_considered[3]{name,rejected_because}:
      Abstract base class hierarchy,Over-engineered for two providers; registry pattern is simpler
      LiteLLM or similar library,Adds external dependency; violates air-gap constraint for offline use
      OpenAI-compatible only (both providers use OpenAI format),Anthropic API format differs significantly; would require Anthropic proxy
    consequences:
      positive[5]: Users can choose Anthropic or OpenAI models via config,"Custom base URL enables Azure OpenAI, Ollama, vLLM support",Backward compatible — missing provider field defaults to anthropic,ai_classifier.py simplified (183 → 130 lines) by extracting API plumbing,Easy to add new providers (implement call/ENV_VAR/DEFAULT_BASE_URL)
      negative[2]: "One additional Python file (ai_providers.py, ~175 lines)","Two SDKs to conditionally install (anthropic, openai)"
    implementation: "New file: .github/actions/scn-detector/scripts/ai_providers.py\n  - AnthropicProvider: SDK + HTTP, x-api-key header, /v1/messages endpoint\n  - OpenAIProvider: SDK + HTTP, Bearer token, /v1/chat/completions endpoint\n  - PROVIDERS registry, create_provider factory, resolve_api_key helper\n\nModified: ai_classifier.py\n  - Uses create_provider() instead of direct Anthropic calls\n  - Provider-agnostic: self.provider.call(prompt)\n\nModified: classify_changes.py\n  - Dynamic API key resolution per provider\n  - Provider-specific env var in warning messages\n\nModified: action.yml\n  - Installs openai SDK when OPENAI_API_KEY is set\n  - Passes both API keys to classify step\n\nConfig: scn-config.yml ai_fallback section\n  provider: \"anthropic\" | \"openai\"\n  model: \"<provider-specific model name>\"\n  api_base_url: \"<optional custom endpoint>\"\n"
    supersedes: Refines ADR-010 (which assumed Anthropic-only)
  - id: ADR-012
    title: "Dual dependency scanning: OSV-Scanner + dependency-review-action"
    date: 2026-03-11
    status: accepted
    context: "Argus lacked source-code dependency vulnerability scanning. Container-level\nscanning (Trivy/Grype/Syft) catches OS package vulnerabilities but misses\napplication dependency vulnerabilities in lockfiles (npm, pip, cargo, etc.).\nSupply chain attacks increasingly target application dependencies.\n"
    decision: "Add two complementary dependency scanners mirroring the Trivy/Grype dual-scanner\npattern for containers:\n1. scanner-osv: Google OSV-Scanner binary — any trigger, GHES-compatible,\n   scans lockfiles against the OSV database\n2. scanner-dependency-review: GitHub's dependency-review-action — PR-only,\n   compares dependency diffs, supports license compliance checking\n"
    alternatives_considered[4]{name,rejected_because}:
      OSV-Scanner only,Misses PR diff context and license compliance checking
      dependency-review-action only,PR-only limitation; no scheduled or push-triggered scanning
      Snyk or commercial tool,Adds external dependency; violates air-gap constraint
      npm audit / pip-audit per-ecosystem,Fragmented; would need separate actions per ecosystem
    consequences:
      positive[5]: Complete dependency vulnerability coverage (any trigger + PR diff),License compliance checking via dependency-review-action,GHES-compatible via OSV binary install (no marketplace dependency),Zero changes to security-summary (artifact-driven aggregation),Mirrors proven Trivy/Grype dual-scanner pattern
      negative[2]: Two scanners to maintain for dependency category,dependency-review-action limited to PR events only
    implementation: "New actions:\n  .github/actions/scanner-osv/\n  .github/actions/scanner-dependency-review/\n\nBoth follow established patterns (ADR-009 Python scripts, ADR-007 severity\nthresholds, ADR-008 flexible PR commenting). scanner-dependency-review\nemits a warning annotation and zero-count summary on non-PR events for\ngraceful degradation.\n"